# Lifecycle Stage

The project's lifecycle fundamentally shapes AI behavior. Early stages prioritize speed; later stages prioritize stability.

## Options

LifecycleStages {
  PoC => Validate idea quickly, likely throwaway
  Throwaway => Solve immediate problem, author-only
  TeamTool => Small known group, needs to be understandable
  MVP => First version for real users
  EarlyProduction => Real users depending on it, growing
  MatureProduction => Stable, reliable, battle-tested
  Legacy => Maintain but not actively developing
}

## Derived Behaviors

| Stage | Errors | Testing | Security | Docs | Refactoring |
|-------|--------|---------|----------|------|-------------|
| PoC | Minimal | Skip | Skip | None | Never |
| Throwaway | Basic | Rare | Basic | None | Never |
| TeamTool | Reasonable | Core paths | Standard | Brief | If helpful |
| MVP | User-facing | Critical | No holes | Deploy | Track debt |
| EarlyProd | Comprehensive | Good | Review | Maintain | Considered |
| Mature | Thorough | High | Paramount | Complete | Careful |
| Legacy | Preserve | Add first | Audit | Discover | Minimal |

## AI Behavior Calibration

BehaviorsByStage {
  PoC => simplest implementation, push back on concerns, no tests unless asked
  Throwaway => "does it work" over "is it clean", skip refactors
  TeamTool => clear naming, brief comments, tests for complex paths
  MVP => insist on error handling, flag security issues, accept "good enough"
  EarlyProd => thorough errors, recommend tests, warn about breaking changes
  Mature => insist on tests, security review, consider rollback
  Legacy => read first, test before change, preserve patterns, small changes
}
